00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:42.600
They could swat the lizard like the flies he's hoping to ambush.

00:42:42.600 --> 00:43:00.840
He will need to pick his target carefully.

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:03.840
Not her.

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:07.800
Or her.

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:12.800
Maybe.

00:43:12.800 --> 00:43:26.440
But no.

00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:35.720
To be within striking distance, he's got to hold his nerve.

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:44.040
Not one.

00:43:44.040 --> 00:44:00.320
Now he's getting his eye in.

00:44:00.320 --> 00:44:23.080
So this might be a bit ambitious.

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:33.960
It may take courage to hunt on the back of a lion.

00:44:33.960 --> 00:44:42.960
But it takes sense to know when to run away.

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:52.000
The wildebeest won't stay for long and when they leave, most of the flies will follow.

00:44:52.000 --> 00:45:02.960
The boom and bust of Africa's savannahs forces animals to gamble with their lives.

00:45:02.960 --> 00:45:13.200
To the north, the power of Africa's greatest desert makes life almost impossible.

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:19.680
The merciless sun, a colossal 15 million degree nuclear reactor, scorches life from the surface

00:45:19.680 --> 00:45:26.320
of the land.

00:45:26.320 --> 00:45:50.240
A faint breath of wind can be the beginning of disaster.

00:45:50.240 --> 00:46:15.240
A

00:46:15.240 --> 00:46:25.480
sandstorm can be a thousand miles across.

00:46:25.480 --> 00:46:36.840
The constant battering of the land wears down the hard-baked ground.

00:46:36.840 --> 00:46:44.640
The landscape is littered with giant chalk pillars carved by innumerable sandstorms.

00:46:44.640 --> 00:46:55.240
This glaringly white oven is lethally hot.

00:46:55.240 --> 00:47:14.120
It's 41 degrees, too much for most animals.

00:47:14.120 --> 00:47:21.160
The sun and sandstorms cannot, however, reach far underground, where conditions are stable

00:47:21.160 --> 00:47:29.680
and tolerable.

00:47:29.680 --> 00:47:44.080
Meet one of the planet's strangest mammals, naked mole rats.

00:47:44.080 --> 00:47:50.760
These saber-toothed sausages wouldn't last a day in the desert.

00:47:50.760 --> 00:47:55.160
Special filming tunnels allow us to see how well adapted they are to the subterranean

00:47:55.160 --> 00:47:56.160
life.

00:47:56.160 --> 00:48:03.280
They can run equally well in both directions, so tight space is no problem.

00:48:03.280 --> 00:48:07.240
They have lost their fur.

00:48:07.240 --> 00:48:17.520
And most bizarrely, they live in social colonies, much like termites or ants.

00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:24.040
After time spent digging, the workers come together to relax.

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:35.560
But one here is very different from all the rest.

00:48:35.560 --> 00:48:48.000
Their queen, twice as heavy as her subjects, and not afraid to throw her weight around.

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:57.880
She is the mother of every worker in the colony and exists in a near-continuous state of pregnancy.

00:48:57.880 --> 00:49:08.840
And now two dozen babies are pulsating within her swollen belly.

00:49:08.840 --> 00:49:14.360
Just occasionally, one of her brood is raised differently.

00:49:14.360 --> 00:49:20.840
A daughter becomes a princess.

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:33.280
Until now, this youngster's enjoyed a lazy privilege life, but not for much longer.

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:43.480
She has a destiny to fulfill.

00:49:43.480 --> 00:49:55.720
The surface is a place where no naked mole rat can survive for long.

00:49:55.720 --> 00:50:09.160
But a princess will risk everything to search for a partner.

00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:18.880
The quest is urgent.

00:50:18.880 --> 00:50:30.680
There's an enticing smell in the air.

00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:45.240
The seductive scent draws her downwards to safety.

00:50:45.240 --> 00:50:48.960
She's sniffed out a partner.

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:59.760
He, too, is alone and eager to start a new colony in his lonely burrow.

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:14.000
A few months later, the princess has become a queen, and a new tyranny begins.

00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:43.280
Above ground, life continues to be tough.

00:51:43.280 --> 00:51:45.200
It's approaching midday.

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:50.880
A fringe-toed lizard is hungry.

00:51:50.880 --> 00:52:06.680
He's on a stakeout.

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:13.720
Flashy scales reflect some of the sun's rays.

00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:20.520
Nevertheless, the heat is almost unbearable.

00:52:20.520 --> 00:52:31.000
His prey hasn't left home all day.

00:52:31.000 --> 00:52:34.880
He can't take the heat anymore.

00:52:34.880 --> 00:52:38.520
To survive longer, you'll need a space suit.

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:48.760
And in a way, that's what these insects have.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:53.040
Silver ants' armored skin reflects light.

00:52:53.040 --> 00:52:59.680
They can tolerate temperatures that would kill any other land animal.

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:14.400
Even so, they can only survive for less than 10 minutes in the midday sun.

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:16.960
Time is precious.

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:30.380
The ants race to find food as soon as their predators go to ground.

00:53:30.380 --> 00:53:35.560
They can't afford to waste a second getting lost, so they spin to take a bearing from

00:53:35.560 --> 00:53:37.880
the sun.

00:53:37.880 --> 00:53:42.960
They log every change of direction, every footstep, in order to know exactly where they

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:51.880
are and where their nest lies.

00:53:51.880 --> 00:53:56.960
Only four minutes to spare, and they found a victim of heat stroke.

00:53:56.960 --> 00:54:01.720
A meal.

00:54:01.720 --> 00:54:19.640
But it's going to take a monumental effort to get it home.

00:54:19.640 --> 00:54:25.120
Three minutes to go, and they're nearing their maximum temperature, an astounding 53 degrees

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:35.240
Celsius.

00:54:35.240 --> 00:54:47.260
But there are already casualties.

00:54:47.260 --> 00:54:50.520
One minute left, and they're not going to make it.

00:54:50.520 --> 00:54:59.200
They must lighten their load.

00:54:59.200 --> 00:55:21.500
The ants have come full circle.

00:55:21.500 --> 00:55:39.820
The

00:55:39.820 --> 00:55:45.980
silver ant is the hardiest of all the desert's inhabitants, maybe even the hardiest of all

00:55:45.980 --> 00:55:59.020
Africa's creatures. From the Sahara to the southern tip and all across Africa, animals

00:55:59.020 --> 00:56:12.740
have been forced to adapt and behave in extraordinary ways. But life here is astonishingly resilient.

00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:18.340
And it is Africa's most successful and adaptable species that is having the biggest impact

00:56:18.340 --> 00:56:32.460
on the continent, with the arrival of agriculture and the growth of big cities. Africa's human

00:56:32.460 --> 00:56:44.380
population is growing at nearly double the global rate. It has just exceeded one billion.

00:56:44.380 --> 00:56:51.620
The future for Africa's wild places and wildlife is uncertain. The pressures on the land and

00:56:51.620 --> 00:57:04.020
the challenges ahead are formidable. There is an increasing urgency to understand and

00:57:04.020 --> 00:57:19.500
crucially to conserve the celebrated wildlife of this great continent.

00:57:19.500 --> 00:57:29.100
Nobody knows what the future has in store for this little calf, nor indeed what changes

00:57:29.100 --> 00:57:42.060
will take place on the great continent on which he lives. But what we do know is that

00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:50.240
human beings have lived alongside wildlife here for longer than anywhere else. And Africa

00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:59.860
is still the greatest wilderness on earth. We don't yet know all there is to know about

00:57:59.860 --> 00:58:15.060
Africa and there is still plenty to explore and enjoy. But critically, there is still

00:58:15.060 --> 00:58:32.940
plenty that must be protected if it is to have a future.
